= Turing machine — Technical =

== Formal model ==
A deterministic single-tape Turing machine can be represented by finite state set Q, tape alphabet Γ, input alphabet Σ, and partial transition function such as:

<pre>
δ : Q × Γ → Q × Γ × {L,R}
</pre>

with designated start and halting/acceptance conventions.

== Configurations ==
A configuration records current state, tape contents and head position.

A computation is a sequence of configurations related by δ.

== Equivalence ==
Multi-tape, nondeterministic and many other Turing-machine variants have equivalent computability power, though simulation can alter complexity.

Lambda calculus and general recursive functions are extensionally equivalent models of computability.

== Universal simulation ==
A universal machine U can satisfy schematically:

<pre>
U(<M>,x) = M(x)
</pre>

when M halts on x.

== Undecidability ==
The halting set:

<pre>
HALT = {(<M>,x) : M halts on x}
</pre>

is not decidable by any Turing machine.

